## 1. Taxonomy Findings Summary (for prompt embedding)

### F1: Bridger Gateway Effect
> In the 22-case corpus (61 edges), **82.0%** of all propagation edges originate 
> from a Bridger source. Of these, **92.0%** start from root source nodes (N0). 
> Government/official agencies account for **39.3%** of all source platforms.
> These Bridgers provide the raw material that all subsequent actors modify.

### F2: Asymmetric Role Transitions
> - Bridger → Amplifier is the dominant transition (**34.4%**, 21/61 edges)
> - Bridger → Bridger is the second most common (**24.6%**, 15/61 — legitimacy transfer)
> - Bridger → Extender is the third (**19.7%**, 12/61)
> - Amplifier can chain to Amplifier (**8.2%**, 5/61), creating cumulative 
>   minor shifts (60% of A→A shifts are minor)
> - Extender → Amplifier (**3.3%**) creates a two-step reframing chain
> - Adverser as target sometimes happens

### F3: Role-Operation Signatures
> - **Amplifiers** primarily use text-layer (47.6%) and visual-layer (39.7%) operations: 
>   `add annotation` (17×), `change color` (13×), `change title` (9×) — 
>   "change the packaging, not the data" (data-layer only 11.1%)
> - **Extenders** have the highest data-layer proportion (32.0%): 
>   `add data variables` (6×) is their signature operation, combined with 
>   `change title` (5×) and `add annotation` to extend larger issues(5×)
> - **Adversers** use selective data operations combined with source removal: 
>   `select time range` (2×), `delete source` (1×), `add annotation` (2×) — 
>   "reshape the evidence base" with cross-layer operations
> - **Bridgers** when modifying use mainly visual changes (50.0%): color adjustments 
>   for platform consistency, minimal data manipulation

### F4: Cross-Platform Authority Gradient
> Platform tiers: T1 (Gov/Research) → T2 (News/3rd-party/Org Blog) → T3 (Social/UGC/Personal)
> - Downward flow dominates: **50.8%** of edges flow from higher to lower tiers
> - T2→T3 transfers produce **100% significant** shifts
> - T1→T3 (two-tier drop): **57.1% significant**
> - Same-tier transfers are bimodal: 42.3% minor, 53.8% significant — 
>   role transition mediates shift within same tier
> - **Platform source-sink polarity**: Gov/Official is a strong net source (+20), 
>   Social Media (-8) and Online Community (-6) are strong net sinks

### F5: Adverser Threshold
> **Major** (extreme) framing shifts occur **only** when an Adverser is involved 
> minor-to-significant levels across all 58 non-adverser edges.

### F6: Operation Complexity Correlates with Shift Magnitude
> - 1 operation: 60.7% minor/none, 39.3% significant
> - 2 operations: 33.3% minor, 66.7% significant
> - 3+ operations: **100% significant or major**
> - Single-layer operations: 58.1% minor/none
> - 2-layer operations: 79.2% significant or major
> - 3-layer operations (data+visual+text): **100% significant or major**
> - Major shifts always involve 3+ operations

### F7: Topology Types
> - Tree topology: **81.8%** (18/22 cases) — divergent, one source fans to multiple variants
> - Linear topology: **18.2%** (4/22 cases) — sequential chain, found in highly 
>   politicized or niche topics (C08 immigration, C14 IQ, C15 housing, C26 fossil fuels)

### F8: Platform Source-Sink Polarity
> - Gov/Official: strong net source (+20 flow difference)
> - Research Inst: weak net source (+3)
> - News Media: moderate net sink (-7), intermediary role
> - Social Media: strong net sink (-8)
> - Online Community: strong net sink (-6)
> - This reveals a structural asymmetry: T1 produces but rarely absorbs; 
>   T3 absorbs and modifies but rarely feeds back upstream

